# Wiring your existing SonarQube install to this bundle

You generated this bundle with `--edition none`, so it does not run SonarQube — it assumes you
already have a zip/host install of SonarQube running, and only starts the containers around it
(database is external in this mode too). These are the changes to make in *your* SonarQube's
`sonar.properties`.

## 1. Database

This bundle points the containers at the same external database you passed to `generate.py`.
Point your SonarQube at it too, if it isn't already:

```
sonar.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://${db_host}:${db_port}/${db_name}${db_url_params}
sonar.jdbc.username=${db_user}
sonar.jdbc.password=<the --db-password you passed to generate.py>
```

The URL above contains `sslmode=verify-full`. That parameter is defined once and the orchestrator
uses it too. SonarQube and the orchestrator accept the connection only when the database serves
TLS, the certificate is valid for `${db_host}`, and the CA that signed it is one they trust. A
server with TLS off is refused. If the orchestrator then exits with
`Failed to initialize database dialect`, the cause further down is
`PKIX path building failed`: the database CA is missing from one of the two places below.

### Zip install

SonarQube runs on the host. Import the database CA into its truststore, and copy the same PEM
into `CUSTOM_CA_DIR` for the orchestrator. Sections 2 to 7 continue this zip install.

SonarQube trusts that CA through a copy of the JDK `cacerts`. When this bundle has TLS, section 7
adds this bundle's CA to the same file, so do not replace the file after this step:

```
cp "$JAVA_HOME/lib/security/cacerts" /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks
chmod u+w /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks
keytool -importcert -noprompt -alias database-ca -file <your database CA certificate> \
  -keystore /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -storepass changeit
```

Then in `sonar.properties`, append these flags to any options you already set on those properties.
If `-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore` already points at a file, import the database CA into that file
instead of the copy above, and use that same file for the import in section 7. SonarQube reads
the properties at startup (section 6):

```
sonar.web.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=/opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
sonar.ce.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=/opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
```

Copy the same CA, a PEM file whose name ends in `.crt` or `.pem`, into the directory
`CUSTOM_CA_DIR` in this bundle's `.env` before `docker compose up -d`. The orchestrator reads it
from there. The certificate needs a `keyUsage` extension that includes `keyCertSign`. If
`CUSTOM_CA_DIR` is unset, regenerate with `--custom-ca-dir` pointing at a directory that contains
the database CA.

### Bundled SonarQube

A private CA is not supported.

